@node1 = http://localhost:5001
@node2 = http://localhost:5002

### 1. Node 1 wallet (its address is also the miner address)
GET {{node1}}/wallet

### 2. Connect the nodes to each other (both directions)
POST {{node1}}/peers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "url": "http://localhost:5002" }

###
POST {{node2}}/peers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "url": "http://localhost:5001" }

### 3. Node 1 mines a block and earns the reward; node 2 syncs by itself
POST {{node1}}/mine

### 4. Node 2 now has the same chain (2 blocks: genesis + block 1)
GET {{node2}}/chain

### 5. Copy node 2's address...
GET {{node2}}/wallet

### 6. ...and paste it in "to": node 1 pays node 2 (the transaction is gossiped)
POST {{node1}}/wallet/send
Content-Type: application/json

{ "to": "PASTE_NODE2_ADDRESS_HERE", "amount": 20 }

### 7. Node 2 received the pending transaction through gossip
GET {{node2}}/pending

### 8. Node 2 mines: confirms the payment and earns a reward; node 1 syncs by itself
POST {{node2}}/mine

###
GET {{node1}}/wallet

###
GET {{node2}}/wallet

### 9. FORK EXPERIMENT. Restart BOTH nodes (Ctrl+C, dotnet run) to wipe everything. Do NOT connect them yet.
### Node 1 mines twice, node 2 mines once: each one believes in its own chain.
POST {{node1}}/mine

###
POST {{node1}}/mine

###
POST {{node2}}/mine

### Now node 2 meets node 1 and runs the consensus: node 1's chain is longer (3 vs 2).
POST {{node2}}/peers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "url": "http://localhost:5001" }

###
POST {{node2}}/resolve

### Node 2 abandoned its own block: its reward is gone (balance 0) and its chain equals node 1's.
GET {{node2}}/wallet

###
GET {{node2}}/status